(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-supraventricular-arrhythmias-quiz"]={"title":"Arrhythmias I: Supraventricular Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A junctional rhythm due to sick sinus syndrome or high-grade atrioventricular block is treated with what?","choices":["Adenosine","Catheter ablation","Permanent pacemaker","Observation alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Management of junctional rhythm depends on the cause, and when it is due to complete or high-grade atrioventricular block or sick sinus syndrome, a permanent pacemaker is placed. Digoxin toxicity is instead treated with atropine and/or digoxin immune Fab (fragment antigen-binding), and asymptomatic healthy people need no treatment. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Why is adenosine useful when focal atrial tachycardia is suspected?","choices":["It reliably ends the focus","It is the long-term cure","It prevents recurrences","Its failure is diagnostic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Adenosine is an important diagnostic tool when focal atrial tachycardia is suspected because it is usually less effective at terminating it than the reentry tachycardias that depend on the atrioventricular node. A tachycardia that persists despite adenosine therefore points toward an atrial focus. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which is a listed risk factor for atrial fibrillation?","choices":["Sleep apnea","Hypothyroidism","Low body weight","Young age"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sleep apnea is a listed risk; atrial fibrillation is commonly seen in patients with hypertension, diabetes, cardiac disease, a family history of atrial fibrillation, obesity, sleep apnea, excessive alcohol and older age. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the first-line treatment for stable supraventricular tachycardia?","choices":["Adenosine","Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous procainamide","A vagal maneuver"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Stable supraventricular tachycardia is treated first with a vagal maneuver. Adenosine follows, and may be repeated once, to convert the rhythm or slow it enough to see P waves; a hemodynamically unstable patient instead gets immediate synchronized cardioversion. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A slow sinus rhythm that is causing hypotension and confusion is treated first with which drug?","choices":["Metoprolol","Digoxin","Atropine","Adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A slow sinus rhythm causing hypotension and confusion is acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable bradycardia, and atropine is first line. If it fails, a temporary pacemaker follows; chronic symptomatic bradycardia is treated with a permanent pacemaker instead. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"In tachy-brady syndrome, which tachyarrhythmia is most common?","choices":["Atrial flutter","Ventricular tachycardia","Sinus tachycardia","Atrial fibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tachy-brady syndrome is the form of sick sinus syndrome in which a tachyarrhythmia alternates with symptomatic bradycardia or offset pauses. The most common cause of the fast phase is atrial fibrillation, which appears as an irregularly irregular rhythm without P waves. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the usual rate of atrial fibrillation?","choices":["40 to 60","60 to 100","110 to 160","250 to 300"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atrial fibrillation usually runs at 110 to 160, though not always: the rate can fall below 80 or rise above 200. The rhythm is irregularly irregular with absent P waves, no PR interval and a narrow QRS. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"In whom are the symptoms of supraventricular tachycardia more pronounced?","choices":["Elderly patients or heart disease","Young, healthy athletes","Those with paroxysmal episodes","Women more than men"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms of supraventricular tachycardia, such as palpitations, lightheadedness, dyspnea and chest discomfort, may be more pronounced in the elderly and in those with underlying structural heart disease. Many patients are otherwise asymptomatic or have only transient symptoms. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which physical finding fits atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia?","choices":["Absent leg pulses","Neck pulsations","Kussmaul sign","Pulsus paradoxus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patients with atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia typically complain of sudden-onset palpitations and neck pulsations, seen on examination as the frog sign. The rhythm is a regular, narrow tachycardia with hidden P waves from a reentry circuit within the node. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What defines long-standing persistent atrial fibrillation?","choices":["Continuous for 7 days to a year","Episodes lasting under a week","Agreeing to stop rhythm control","Continuous for over a year"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Long-standing persistent atrial fibrillation is continuous for more than one year. Paroxysmal lasts under a week and usually ends without treatment, persistent is continuous for over 7 days but under a year and needs treatment, and permanent is defined by a joint decision rather than by duration. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia is more common in whom?","choices":["Men","People with lung disease","People with heart surgery","Women"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia affects women far more than men and is commonly seen without heart disease. It may occur spontaneously or with exertion, caffeine, alcohol, beta-agonists or sympathomimetics. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"How is a junctional rhythm generally tolerated?","choices":["Well","Poorly, with syncope","It degenerates to fibrillation","Usually needs a pacemaker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A junctional rhythm is generally well tolerated. Management depends on the cause: no treatment for an asymptomatic, otherwise healthy person, a permanent pacemaker for complete or high-grade block or sick sinus syndrome, and atropine and/or digoxin immune Fab (fragment antigen-binding) for digitalis toxicity. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which is a listed cause of junctional rhythm?","choices":["Anemia","Digoxin toxicity","Hyperthyroidism","Heavy caffeine use"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin toxicity is a listed cause of junctional rhythm, along with sinus node dysfunction such as sick sinus syndrome or significant bradycardia, inferior myocardial infarction and the acute period after cardiac surgery. Junctional rhythm from digitalis toxicity is treated with atropine and/or digoxin immune Fab (fragment antigen-binding). — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"For stable, symptomatic focal atrial tachycardia, which acute drugs are used?","choices":["Intravenous atropine","Intravenous magnesium","Intravenous procainamide","Intravenous rate control"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hemodynamically stable, symptomatic focal atrial tachycardia is treated acutely with an intravenous beta blocker or nondihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, while the underlying cause or precipitating factors are found and treated. Synchronized cardioversion is kept for instability, and amiodarone may be tried for chemical cardioversion. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Besides drugs and thyroid disease, which condition is a listed cause of sinus bradycardia?","choices":["Pain or anxiety","Hypoxia","Intracranial hypertension","Pulmonary embolism"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Increased intracranial pressure is a listed cause of sinus bradycardia, together with sinus node ischemia, late-stage infection, hypothyroidism and the drugs beta blockers, nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers and digoxin. Sinus bradycardia can also be a normal variant in athletes, during sleep and in adults over 65. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which P wave finding fits atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia?","choices":["Hidden or retrograde P waves","Sawtooth flutter waves","Three or more P-wave shapes","One upright P before each QRS"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia the P waves are hidden or retrograde and the PR interval is hidden or short, in a regular rhythm above 100 and usually 200 to 300. In the orthodromic form the retrograde P wave falls after a normal-width QRS. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which condition is a pathologic cause of sinus bradycardia?","choices":["Hypovolemia","Hypothyroidism","Hyperthyroidism","Anemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hypothyroidism is a listed cause of sinus bradycardia, with sinus node ischemia, increased intracranial pressure, late-stage infection and drugs such as beta blockers, nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers and digoxin. Sinus bradycardia can be either pathological or a normal variant. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Persistent, symptomatic premature atrial contractions are treated first with which?","choices":["Amiodarone","A beta blocker","Adenosine","Procainamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Persistent, symptomatic premature atrial contractions are treated with a beta blocker such as metoprolol if not contraindicated. Catheter ablation is reserved for frequent beats or beats triggering atrial fibrillation, and asymptomatic ones need no treatment. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"In sinus arrhythmia, how does the heart rate change with breathing?","choices":["Unchanged; the P-wave shape varies","Faster on inspiration, slower on expiration","Slower on inspiration, faster on expiration","Faster in both phases of the breath"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sinus arrhythmia is a normal, physiologic rhythm that is irregular with a pattern: the rate speeds up with inspiration and slows with expiration. Every beat keeps a uniform P wave and a narrow QRS, and the rate stays within 60 to 100. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"How is hemodynamically stable atrial flutter treated first?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Catheter ablation","Repeated adenosine","Beta or calcium channel blocker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hemodynamically stable atrial flutter is treated first with rate control using a beta blocker or calcium channel blocker, while an unstable patient gets synchronized cardioversion. Rate control is harder to achieve in flutter than in atrial fibrillation, and anticoagulation is part of management. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which drug is a direct oral anticoagulant preferred in nonvalvular atrial fibrillation?","choices":["Procainamide","Apixaban","Warfarin","Aspirin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Apixaban is one of the direct oral anticoagulants preferred in nonvalvular atrial fibrillation, along with dabigatran, edoxaban and rivaroxaban. Patients typically require anticoagulation unless bleeding risk is high, and stroke risk is assessed with the CHA2DS2-VASc (stroke-risk) score. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Adenosine slows a tachycardia and reveals atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White. Which drug is used?","choices":["Intravenous atropine","Intravenous magnesium","Intravenous procainamide","A second dose of adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Slowing from adenosine has unmasked atrial fibrillation conducting with Wolff-Parkinson-White, and in that setting intravenous procainamide is used, never adenosine. The same rule applies whenever Wolff-Parkinson-White is already known; adenosine stays first line only for ordinary orthodromic reentry. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What defines inappropriate sinus tachycardia?","choices":["Tachycardia caused by fever","Tachycardia during exercise","Tachycardia from a stimulant","Unexplained once causes excluded"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Inappropriate sinus tachycardia is an elevated heart rate that remains unexplained after secondary causes have been excluded, so it is a diagnosis of exclusion. It is generally left untreated, because patients tolerate the symptoms better than the treatment and the prognosis is usually benign. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is the tachy-brady form of sick sinus syndrome?","choices":["Slowing on expiration, speeding up on inspiration","Fast atrial rhythms alternating with slow ones","An impulse that cannot exit the node","An impulse the node fails to generate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In tachy-brady syndrome, fast atrial rhythms alternate with slow rhythms or pauses: a tachyarrhythmia, most commonly atrial fibrillation, alternates with symptomatic bradycardia or offset pauses. It is one of the three forms of sick sinus syndrome, with sinus exit block and sinus arrest. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which medication class is a recognized cause of sinus bradycardia?","choices":["Stimulants","Beta-agonist inhalers","Beta blockers","Anticholinergics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers are a listed medication cause of sinus bradycardia, along with nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers and digoxin. The non-drug causes include hypothyroidism, sinus node ischemia, increased intracranial pressure and late-stage infection. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the usual rate of a junctional rhythm?","choices":["40 to 60","60 to 100","100 to 160","250 to 300"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A junctional rhythm is regular, usually at 40 to 60, with a narrow QRS under 0.12 seconds and P waves that are inverted in II, III and aVF before the QRS, buried in it or after it, with a short or absent PR. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which counseling fits benign premature atrial contractions?","choices":["Begin anticoagulation","Take a lifelong beta blocker","Avoid triggers; reassure","Stop exercising"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. For premature atrial contractions in a normal heart, counsel the patient to avoid potential precipitants such as excessive alcohol, stress and caffeine, and give reassurance about the benign nature of the rhythm. Most people are asymptomatic, and no treatment is needed if there are no symptoms. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which therapy is usually curative for focal atrial tachycardia?","choices":["A permanent pacemaker","Atropine","Catheter ablation","Repeated adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Catheter ablation is usually curative for focal atrial tachycardia, in about 90 percent. For chronic management an oral beta blocker or nondihydropyridine calcium channel blocker is the initial therapy, but ablation is the step that cures the ectopic focus. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which rhythm describes atrial fibrillation?","choices":["Irregularly irregular, no P waves","Regular, with sawtooth waves","Irregular, three P-wave shapes","Regular, with hidden P waves"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Atrial fibrillation is irregularly irregular with absent P waves and an absent PR interval, with a narrow QRS. Its rate is usually 110 to 160 but not always, and can fall below 80 or rise above 200, so the rhythm and the missing P waves identify it, not the rate. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which type of atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia is more common?","choices":["Orthodromic","Antidromic","Multifocal","Slow-fast"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia comes in orthodromic and antidromic forms, and orthodromic is more common. Orthodromic reentry uses antegrade conduction through the atrioventricular node, giving a regular, narrow complex tachycardia with a retrograde P wave after the QRS. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 33"}]};
